-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTS (Most Frequently Asked)
-- ============================================================================

/*
Q1: Explain Snowflake's architecture.
─────────────────────────────────────
A: Snowflake has a unique 3-layer shared-data architecture:
   1. Storage Layer - centralized on cloud object storage (S3/Blob/GCS),
      data stored in columnar, compressed micro-partitions (50-500 MB each).
   2. Compute Layer - independent virtual warehouses (VMs clusters) that
      can scale up/down and out. Multiple warehouses access same data simultaneously.
   3. Cloud Services Layer - brain of the system: authentication, metadata,
      query optimization, transactions, security. Always running.

   Key advantage: Storage and compute scale INDEPENDENTLY. You can have
   10 warehouses reading the same data without copying it.


Q2: What are micro-partitions?
──────────────────────────────
A: Immutable, compressed columnar storage units (50-500 MB uncompressed).
   Snowflake auto-creates them as data is loaded. Each partition stores
   metadata (min/max values, distinct count, null count) which enables
   "partition pruning" — skipping irrelevant partitions during queries.
   Think of it like automatic indexing without any management.


Q3: Difference between Clustering and Indexing?
──────────────────────────────────────────────
A: Snowflake has NO traditional indexes.
   • Clustering Key = defines how data is physically ORDERED across micro-partitions
   • Benefits: improves partition pruning for large tables (>1TB)
   • Auto-maintained (Automatic Clustering service re-clusters as data changes)
   • Cost: maintenance uses serverless credits
   • When to use: large tables filtered frequently on specific columns (date, region)


Q4: What is Zero-Copy Cloning?
──────────────────────────────
A: CREATE TABLE clone_table CLONE source_table;
   Creates an instant copy that references the same micro-partitions.
   • No additional storage cost until data diverges
   • Works on tables, schemas, and databases
   • Use cases: dev/test environments, pre-deployment backups, experimentation
   • Also supports Time Travel cloning: CLONE ... AT(TIMESTAMP => ...)


Q5: Time Travel vs Fail-Safe?
─────────────────────────────
A: Time Travel:
   • User-accessible, query historical data (AT/BEFORE clauses)
   • Retention: 0-1 day (Standard), 0-90 days (Enterprise)
   • Use: UNDROP, clone from point-in-time, audit

   Fail-Safe:
   • NOT user-accessible (Snowflake support only)
   • Fixed 7-day period AFTER Time Travel expires
   • Disaster recovery only
   • Cost: additional storage for 7 days

   Storage cost = Active + Time Travel + Fail-Safe


Q6: Types of tables and when to use each?
──────────────────────────────────────────
A: • Permanent (default): full Time Travel + Fail-Safe. Use for production data.
   • Transient: Time Travel (0-1 day), NO Fail-Safe. Use for staging, ETL intermediates.
   • Temporary: session-scoped, no TT, no FS. Use for ad-hoc analysis within a session.
   • External: data remains outside Snowflake (your S3). Read-only, no TT/FS.
   • Dynamic: auto-refreshed based on a defining query and target lag.


Q7: Explain Virtual Warehouses and Multi-cluster.
─────────────────────────────────────────────────
A: Virtual Warehouse = a cluster of compute resources (CPU, memory, SSD cache).
   • Sizes: XS (1 node) to 6XL (512 nodes). Each size up = 2x resources, 2x cost.
   • Multi-cluster: auto-scales OUT (adds more clusters) for concurrency.
     - STANDARD policy: spins up cluster as soon as queue forms
     - ECONOMY policy: waits until 6-min load threshold (saves credits)
   • Scale UP for complex queries; Scale OUT for many concurrent queries.


Q8: Streams vs Dynamic Tables?
──────────────────────────────
A: Streams:
   • Track row-level changes (CDC) on a source table
   • Imperative: YOU write the merge/insert logic
   • Consumed on DML (offset advances after successful read)
   • Best for: complex transformations, conditional processing

   Dynamic Tables:
   • Declarative: define WHAT result you want, Snowflake handles HOW/WHEN
   • Set TARGET_LAG for freshness (e.g., '1 hour')
   • Auto-incremental refresh
   • Best for: straightforward transformations, replacing Stream+Task chains
   • Limitation: can't do imperative logic (conditionals, loops)
*/


